tered stones, but no immediate lethal threats (no open 3s or 4s). White\'s most promising strategy is to utilize the diagonal potential to force a move and simultaneously build strength on Row 6.\n\n**Reason:**\nThe move at **E6** is a forcing move (Sente).\n1.  **Immediate Threat:** Placing a stone at **E6** extends the diagonal F7-G8-H9 to form a 4-in-a-row (**E6-F7-G8-H9**). This forces Black to block immediately at **D5** to prevent White from making 5.\n2.  **Strategic Gain:** After the forced exchange (White E6, Black D5), White will have a stone at E6. This stone connects horizontally with the existing stone at F6.\n3.  **Follow-up:** White can then play **G6**. This would create an **Open 3** on Row 6 (E6-F6-G6) and simultaneously a **Sleeping 3** on the diagonal (E8-F7-G6), creating a powerful multi-pronged attack that is very difficult for Black to defend. Playing G6 first would be a mistake, as Black could defend at D5, neutralizing White\'s diagonal threat and taking the initiative.\n\nAnswer:\n{\n  "col": "E",\n  "row": 6\n}'}
