ow or open-3). White has a tactical opportunity to force a win by creating simultaneous threats ("4-3" tactic).\n\nAnalysis of the winning move (F5):\n1.  **Vertical Threat (Column F):** White already has stones at F6, F7, and F8. Playing at **F5** creates a connected line of 4 stones (F5-F6-F7-F8). Since F9 is blocked by a Black stone, this is a "closed 4". It threatens an immediate win at F4. This forces Black to play at F4 to block.\n2.  **Diagonal Threat:** White has stones at D7 and E6. Playing at **F5** connects with these to form a diagonal line of 3 stones (D7-E6-F5). Crucially, this diagonal is "open" on both ends (C8 and G4 are empty).\n3.  **Combination:** By playing F5, White creates a "4-3" fork. Black is forced to defend the vertical 4 at F4. After Black blocks at F4, White is free to play at G4 (or C8) to extend the diagonal 3 into an **Open 4** (e.g., D7-E6-F5-G4). An Open 4 is an unstoppbale win because it creates two winning spots (C8 and H3), and Black can only block one.\n\nReason: The move at F5 creates a "Four-Three" attack. It forms a vertical 4-in-a-row that forces the opponent to block, while simultaneously building a diagonal Open 3. Once the opponent blocks the vertical threat, White converts the diagonal 3 into an Open 4, guaranteeing a win on the following turn.\n\nAnswer: { "col": "F", "row": 5 }'}
