n "Open 4" on the diagonal. It can\'t be stopped, game over! I can\'t be tricked.\n\nI\'m confident I have a winning move.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board shows that White has a strong diagonal line of three stones at D7, E6, and F5. This formation is unblocked on both ends (C8 and G4 are empty), constituting a "Live 3" or "Open 3". The opponent (Black) has stones clustered in the center but possesses no immediate lethal threats (such as an unblocked 4 or Open 3) that require a defensive move from White.\n\nReason: By playing at G4, White extends the diagonal line to form an "Open 4" (D7, E6, F5, G4). An Open 4 is a winning shape because it has two open ends (C8 and H3). The opponent can only block one of these ends in their next turn, which guarantees that White can play the winning 5th stone on the remaining open end in the subsequent turn. C8 is also a valid winning move for the same reason, but G4 is chosen here.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "G", "row": 4}'}
